    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Drasco was originally called Crossroads because it sat at the junction of the Batesville-Clinton and Richwoods-Searcy roads.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/drasco_arkansas",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1864 a Union cavalry detachment surprised about 25 Confederate guerrillas at an old house near Crossroads, routing most of them.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/drasco_arkansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "After the war, settlers flooded into Crossroads, drawn by fertile valleys and wooded hills that would become part of Cleburne County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/drasco_arkansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1917 the community adopted the name Drasco for its post office, supposedly honoring a local Native American group called the Drasco Indians.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/drasco_arkansas",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The lakeside Tannenbaum resort, built in the 1960s, featured chalets, a golf course, a boat ramp, and an airfield, boosting regional tourism.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/drasco_arkansas",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Drasco’s first school, the Comfort School, opened in 1876, marking the town’s early commitment to education.",
